    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates descriptions generated from activating examples against curated biological annotations. Some named contexts do not validate, refining the claim that such descriptions reliably capture a feature's job. This is an indirect test: it does not test prediction of actual held-out model activations.",
    "gate_note": "The closest version is V6, but the study tests description-derived biological context claims against annotations, not whether an explanation predicts a neuron's firing on new text. Intruder detection measures coherence of activating examples, not that prediction. No results test neuron-pole job shares, cross-model shares, InceptionV1 examples, neuron-based attribution graphs, or automated explanation scores on new text. Feature 1652 has no context category available for testing. Results cited from other studies are not recorded.",
    "test_reason": "No version is tested. V6 concerns explanations of neurons, in the language-model sense of the claim, built from activating examples. This study scores LLM descriptions of SAE latents (TopK, 2048 latents) trained on Boltz-1 Pairformer trunk activations, a protein structure model, against SwissProt and DSSP annotations. The unit (SAE latents, not neurons) and the kind of system (protein structure model, not a language model) both differ. The paper never measures neurons or neuron-pole job shares, and it does not cite either original source. The context-validation result is closely related to V6's idea, but it is recorded under fits_no_version, not as a test.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Partly right but incomplete. The main contribution is cross-seed stability as a free prefilter: about 4.4× fewer latent evaluations and 5.2× lower cost per interpretable latent, at about 53% recall. This filter favours structural detectors over functional-site and compositional latents. The authors also recommend the annotation-based ground-truth check as standard practice: the residue part of every description held, while 4 of 16 testable context claims failed, two of them underpowered. They hedge the small sample and say causal tests are needed.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses TopK SAE features in Boltz-1, blind intruder detection, separate LLM-generated biological descriptions, and annotation-based enrichment checks; it does not audit neuron poles or predict neuron activations on new text.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The intruder judge was highly sensitive to output-token allowance: raising it from 16 to 512 tokens changed positive-control accuracy from 0.17 to 0.97. Across prompt phrasings accuracy ranged 0.85–0.97; calibrated variants were 0.957–0.967. These are harness results, not neuron-pole measures.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In Boltz-1 SAE latents, LLM descriptions written from activating windows were checked against annotations. The residue part held for all 17 latents (folds 6.1–84.9×). The named context held for 12 of 16 testable cases, two of the four failures are underpowered, and one 'hold' is vacuous (feature 354).",
        "proposed_claim": "Activating-example descriptions of SAE latents in protein models reliably identify the residue but are less reliable about the restricting context, so a description can fit its examples yet overstate what predicts firing.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R1",
